+<a id="the-session-filter-seat"></a>
+## The session filter seat
+
+`ctx.sessionController.registerSessionFilter(hooks)` claims the single seat of optional session access hooks: a second registration throws because two access policies cannot compose, and the returned disposer releases the seat. Each callback is independent, and an absent callback leaves its seam byte-identical to an unconfigured deployment. `listFilter(principal, items)` rewrites the rows of `list` and the items of `search`; the generic preserves each item type, so summaries and search hits keep their own fields. `accessCheck(principal, sessionId)` admits every Session-addressed method: prompt, cancel, rename, selectModel, attachment, updateQueue, page, fork sources, and follow openings (a subagent address is checked against the child Session it names). `onSessionCreated(principal, sessionId)` is awaited after each successful create or fork, so its claims settle before the response; its rejection is logged and never fails the settled response.
+
+The principal is the request-scoped identity the webserver guard attached to the current request, opaque to the Host. With the matching callback registered, a request without a principal is denied, and a hook that denies or throws is a denial (`logger.warn` on a throw); an anonymous create is rejected before any creation side effect. Every denial is a `TypertRemoteFailure` with the stable code `forbidden`, passed through unchanged by every carrier and consumed by clients with HTTP 403 semantics, plus `{ sessionId }` details where a Session is addressed. `search.hasMore` still describes the provider page before filtering, never the filtered view. `ctx.sessionController.assertSessionAccess(sessionId)` exposes the same admission verdict for any future direct route that addresses a Session.
+
+-----
+
